RELEASE NOTES, c3p0-0.14.2
==========================

This release addresses Statement cache corruption that
appears to result from "dueling Statement caches".

Prior to this release, c3p0's Statement cache simply
presumed that it is handed a freshly created
PreparedStatement when it calls Connection.prepareXXX methods.

But that's not always true. Some Drivers implement
their own Statement caches internally, and nothing prevents
users from accidentally or intentionally running a
configuration in which both c3p0 and the JDBC driver
cache some or all Statements.

Prior to this release, that would lead to corruption
of the Statement cache.

This release makes the Statement cache resilient to that
condition. If the cache, when preparing a "new" Statement,
encounters a reference to a Statement it has already seen,
the cache simply refuses the Statement. The Statement is
made available to the client as an ordinary, uncached
Statement, and its lifecycle is managed like "overload
Statements" -- Statements that could not be cached
because the cache was full.

When c3p0 encounters this condition, it logs a WARNING
naming the likely causes -- a driver-side Statement cache such
as Oracle's implicit statement caching, or a layer that wraps
Connections. If you see it, statement caching remains correct
but is less effective, and we would be glad to hear about it.

(This release also eliminates a potential Statement leak
should clients fail to close the aforementioned "overload
Statements".)

Thanks to Devil2-O on github for reporting conditions
consistent with multiply cached Statements and for helping
us test the fix.
